;; Seon's config: every limit, bound and default of the platform, one key each
;; (owner, 2026-10-21: "the only things that should stay are documented config schemas that we
;; are porting from seon if they are relevant"). v1's shape (~/src/seon config/default.edn):
;; beside each key, its unit, its provenance (an owner ruling, a measurement, the worktree session that set
;; it) and what happens at its bound.
;;
;; - Read once with aero when seon.config loads (root's boot; agents and the fork server are
;;   forked from root and read no environment). Validated against :seon.config/config
;;   (resources/seon/schemas/seon.config*.edn): an unknown key, an invalid value or a missing
;;   required key refuses boot with :seon.config/refused, naming each key.
;; - Root writes the resolved config as facts on main's root entity (platform records, never
;;   carried by a merge): `(seon.config/from-db db)` reads it, on main or any branch.
;; - Overrides come only through #env below, and only where a script or a check sets one, so
;;   this file is the one list of what can be set. A value resolving to nil (an unset #env with
;;   no fallback) is absent, which only an optional key allows.
;; - #profile selects by the command line's --profile NAME (default :default); :deepseek runs agents on DeepSeek.
;; - Machine-dependent values are derived at load, never shipped: #seon/checkout "p" is p under
;;   the checkout this process runs from (SEON_CHECKOUT, else JOLT_PWD).
;; - Layers (owner, 2026-10-21: "hand out different edn config files ... or do command line
;;   overrides"): this file, then the command line's --config FILE and --set :ns/key=edn
;;   (a checkout's config/local.edn, gitignored, which bin/ports writes with its ports, is one
;;   only where a command line names it: bin/root does for the standing root). Later
;;   layers win key by key; the merged map is validated once; seon.config/sources (and the
;;   :seon.config/sources fact on main) names the layer each key came from.
{;; ==== root and its procs ====================================================================

 ;; Unit: live agents. Root holds a socket (two descriptors) per agent and raises its open-file
 ;; limit to 256 + 2 x this at boot. Provenance: proto/flow f2-final's PROTO_MAX_AGENTS (the
 ;; first version forked a thousand agents at ~1.4 MB each, AGENTS.md).
 ;; At the bound: a spawn is refused (:seon.root/refused "too many live agents").
 :seon.config.root/max-agents 1024
 ;; Unit: bytes. How much of a dead agent's stderr its death records: the last bytes of its
 ;; file. Provenance: the value seon.root held as a literal (shrink). At the bound: earlier
 ;; output is not recorded; the file itself is kept.
 :seon.config.root/death-stderr-bytes 2048
 ;; Unit: ms. Flow's :stop-timeout-ms for root's graph (seon.root/start!). Provenance: the literal
 ;; 10000 it replaces. At the bound: flow/stop answers the pids still running.
 :seon.config.root/flow-stop-ms 10000
 ;; Unit: entries. The newest entries root's view keeps of a list it grows: an agent's
 ;; evaluations and root's own defects; the view counts them all, and a render says how many it
 ;; left. Provenance: the value seon.root held as a literal twice (shrink). At the bound: the
 ;; oldest entry leaves the view; the facts on main keep everything.
 :seon.config.root/view-window 20

 ;; Unit: writer requests from the api in flight. Provenance: f2-final. At the bound the api
 ;; stops reading its server (Flow's input filter) and reads only answers, so callers are
 ;; pushed back and the writer's answers always have room: the api <-> writer cycle cannot wedge.
 :seon.config.root/api-writes 512
 ;; Unit: messages one api step takes before it applies its graph change (spawns and ends
 ;; become one update!). Provenance: f2-final. At the bound: the step ends and the rest wait.
 :seon.config.root/api-batch 256
 ;; Unit: requests one writer step drains and commits together (group commit). Provenance:
 ;; f2-final. At the bound: the next step takes the rest.
 :seon.config.root/writer-batch 256
 ;; Unit: requests. The writer's in-port. Each agent has at most one write in flight, so a
 ;; buffer the size of the live agents never fills from agents. At the bound senders park.
 :seon.config.root/writer-buf #ref [:seon.config.root/max-agents]
 ;; Unit: on/off. Whether a load of the wiki takes, for a section with no record of the code it was written
 ;; against (:seon.doc/read), that record from git: the form ids of the vars it cites as of the commit
 ;; that last changed it, so a section already stale shows as such (docs/health.md#a-stale-section). On
 ;; Seon's wiki that is one git blame per page and one git show per (commit, file), 4 s measured
 ;; 2026-10-23 (303 sections, 139 citing code), once per store. Off (bin/test and bin/check set
 ;; SEON_WIKI_BASELINE=false: every test root has a store of its own): such a section is recorded as of today.
 :seon.config.root/wiki-baseline #boolean #or [#env SEON_WIKI_BASELINE true]
 ;; Unit: main's commits queued for the reconciler. It reads every commit's datoms (an index
 ;; kept by observing them), so the buffer blocks rather than drops: at the bound the writer's
 ;; send parks until the reconciler catches up (a pass costs what the commit changed).
 :seon.config.root/reconciler-buf 1024
 ;; Unit: main commits. The fork server forks a fresh standby after this many, bounding the
 ;; log of commits a promoted standby is sent. Provenance: f2-final.
 :seon.config.root/standby-every 256
 ;; Unit: threads, Chez's parallel-collector sweepers in root, fixed at boot right after the
 ;; fork server is forked (seon.lib/gc-sweepers!; seon.main/-main fixed the pool at 0 before that, so the
 ;; fork server never has one). 0: root collects on the thread that triggers
 ;; it. Provenance: session THREADS, 2026-10-21: unfixed, Chez kept one per thread a collection
 ;; found waiting, so root's count drifted with load (0, 5, 15 across identical runs); A/B at
 ;; 0/2/4/8 gave the same GC real time, GC CPU rising with n, threads 37/39/41/45. At the bound: none.
 :seon.config.root/gc-sweepers 0
 ;; Unit: percent, the most of root's heap kept free after a full collection (Jolt's
 ;; JOLT_MAX_HEAP_FREE_RATIO, the JVM's -XX:MaxHeapFreeRatio: P% free is P/(100-P) of the heap in
 ;; use), set after the fork server is forked (seon.lib/max-heap-free-ratio!), so the fork server and
 ;; agents keep Jolt's default 50; it bounds root's nursery headroom over the live set by the same share. Root collects in full at
 ;; boot and at each quiescence (the writer's :collect), which is when Chez returns pages.
 ;; Provenance: jolt/root-reserve-cap (Jolt's default 50 kept 1x, 234-450 MB free in an idle
 ;; root). At the bound: 1 returns nearly every free page and keeps the nursery at its floor.
 :seon.config.root/max-heap-free-ratio 10

 ;; Where kept runs live (owner, 2026-10-21: runs are kept, "a perfect record frozen in time").
 ;; bin/root exports SEON_RUNS (<this tree>/runs).
 :seon.config.root/runs-dir #or [#env SEON_RUNS #seon/checkout "runs"]
 ;; The one store (owner, 2026-10-21: one store, many branches, as in production): created
 ;; when missing; every root works on its own main, a branch of the store's seed. bin/check
 ;; sets SEON_STORE to its run's store.
 :seon.config.root/store-path #or [#env SEON_STORE #join [#ref [:seon.config.root/runs-dir] "/store"]]
 ;; Root's main, as a branch name: one main per store, resumed at every boot (owner,
 ;; 2026-10-22: "why are we not resuming one main by default"); a store without it makes it
 ;; from the seed (:db). A run's record is main's commit history. bin/check names each step's
 ;; own main (SEON_MAIN=check-<step>, deleted when the step is green); SEON_MAIN=db is :db itself.
 ;; Provenance: the branches plan, not kept point 1. At the bound: n/a.
 :seon.config.root/main #or [#env SEON_MAIN "main"]
 ;; Root's system declaration (owner, 2026-10-20: the definition lives in a file).
 :seon.config.root/system-path #seon/checkout "resources/seon/system.edn"
 ;; The task files (resources/seon/tasks/<owning-ns>.edn, AGENTS.md "How work is handed out").
 :seon.config.root/tasks-dir #seon/checkout "resources/seon/tasks"
 ;; Unit: directories of the checkout ("." is its top level, the .md files there: AGENTS.md, BAGGAGE.md,
 ;; README.md). The pages root loads into main as wiki sections at boot (seon.root/wiki-tx), so one search
 ;; and one view reach them (owner, 2026-10-27: "everything of consequence that is for seon agents use
 ;; should be indexed into the db"). docs/ is also watched and reloaded on change (my.root/loader-dirs);
 ;; the top level is read at boot only (watching it would watch the whole checkout, which kept
 ;; fseventsd saturated). Provenance: one-index audit, step 6 (root's scratchpad one-index.md). At the
 ;; bound: n/a; a dir with no .md files loads nothing.
 :seon.config.root/wiki-dirs ["docs" "."]

 ;; ==== the store ==============================================================================

 ;; The durable store's konserve backend: LMDB (one env sync per commit) or the POSIX filestore.
 :seon.config.store/backend :lmdb
 ;; Unit: bytes. LMDB's map size, the store's largest size. At the bound LMDB refuses the
 ;; write (MDB_MAP_FULL, a :seon.writer/refused answer). Provenance: the store's first
 ;; default was 1 GiB (seon.db's SEON_LMDB_MB); on 2026-10-21 the standing root's kept store
 ;; reached 747 MB and two agents died on Datahike's fatal-commit-error while recording. The map
 ;; is address space, not memory or disk: without MDB_WRITEMAP (the flags are MDB_NOSYNC) the
 ;; file grows only as it is used, so LMDB's guidance is a map as large as the store may ever
 ;; grow. 64 GiB.
 :seon.config.store/lmdb-map-bytes 68719476736
 ;; Unit: children per index node. A commit rewrites the nodes it touched, so smaller nodes
 ;; mean fewer bytes printed and written per commit. Provenance: dev/dur.clj measured 512 -> 64
 ;; cut a small commit 27 -> 16 ms with fsync, before the node emitter.
 :seon.config.store/branching-factor 64
 ;; Boolean: whether a store is made keeping history: its temporal indexes (d/history, as-of,
 ;; since; a retraction stays a datom) and each transaction's tx-meta, :db/txInstant included
 ;; (Datahike applies tx-meta only with history). Fixed when the store is made (Datahike's
 ;; store-fixed-record-keys): a kept store opens with what it was made with. Provenance: v1's
 ;; ruling 23 (2026-08-03) and Datahike's default; next had it false, hardcoded, until 2026-10-22.
 ;; Cost measured on seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes (docs/storage.md).
 :seon.config.store/keep-history? true
 ;; Unit: bytes, or :never. A datom value at least this large (a string's characters, a
 ;; collection's elements) is stored once under its own content key in Datahike's store, and
 ;; the index nodes hold a reference to it (datahike.index.persistent-set's out-of-line), so a
 ;; commit rewriting the index tail never writes it again; :never keeps every value inline.
 ;; Provenance: measured 2026-10-22 on seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes
 ;; (docs/storage.md#values-out-of-line); v1 kept values up to 4,096 bytes inline (seon.blob).
 :seon.config.store/value-out-of-line-bytes 1024
 ;; Unit: characters. The largest value an eval's record keeps (seon.repl/record-details): a count over the value by a
 ;; walk that stops once it passes the bound (each scalar 1, a string or keyword its length; nothing is printed to
 ;; measure). At the bound: the value is not kept, its type and this bound are ({:seon.value/gone {:type :over}}),
 ;; the transcript says it is gone, and the handle (result/e<n>) still reads it in the live process.
 ;; Provenance: the owner's ruling, 2026-11-04 ("We only need to store what we need to retrieve"); 1 MiB is a
 ;; round number above any value a reply shows, which the printer cuts to its token budget (docs/storage.md#plain-data).
 :seon.config.store/max-value-chars 1048576
 ;; Codec: konserve's per-value compressor for every stored value (index nodes, values out of
 ;; line, commits): zstd frames at level 3 through libzstd (konserve.jolt.zstd, built from
 ;; reference-code/zstd), :lz4 frames (liblz4), or :none; a value's header names its codec, so a
 ;; store holding several reads them all. Provenance, 2026-10-22: on 5,006 real eval records
 ;; compressed one by one, zstd-3 2.05x against LZ4 1.49x (replies 2.16x vs 1.56x); on
 ;; seon.db-test/a-commit-costs-what-it-writes, 20 per-turn commits 194 KB vs LZ4's 233 KB at the
 ;; same ~1.9 ms a commit. A trained dictionary (7.3x on the records) waits for a representative
 ;; corpus (owner, 2026-10-22).
 :seon.config.store/compressor :zstd
 ;; Unit: bytes. The node cache of a process (Datahike's :store-cache-bytes): index nodes read
 ;; back from the store, kept by address and shared by every connection and branch db of the
 ;; store in that process (datahike.index.interface/node-cache-key), least recently used out
 ;; first (a clock: a hit sets a flag, an eviction gives a flagged oldest node a second chance).
 ;; Jolt's cache weighs each node by what its keys retain (datahike.index.node-cache/node-bytes,
 ;; about 12 KB a leaf here), and a branch keeps no child it restored (persistent-sorted-set
 ;; :weak settings), so the cache is the one holder of nodes read back and this bound is the
 ;; bytes they retain: weighed 134.2 MB against 127.6 MB retained, counted through the static
 ;; generation, on a root that had read every node (it was 1.6-1.8x under before: 125.7 MB
 ;; weighed, 204-226 MB retained). At the bound a node is evicted and its next read goes to the
 ;; store again (65-75 us cold against 8.6 us warm per seek). Provenance, measured 2026-10-29
 ;; (docs/machine.md#roots-heap): a root of main at 200,744 datoms (540,000 index entries) reads
 ;; every node of main at boot and holds 136 MB weighed (16,500 nodes; 7,400 reads, 850,000 hits
 ;; in the next three minutes, no evictions under a 1 GiB bound), so 128 MiB holds 94% of what
 ;; an idle root reads (hits 98.4% before its first eviction). The bound is read by the root,
 ;; the fork server (which reads main once for the agents it forks) and each agent alike: an
 ;; agent inherits its parent's cache and adds what it reads, and the size of that is not yet
 ;; measured (task seon.db/an-agents-node-cache-is-sized-from-what-an-agent-reads).
 ;; Provenance of the size, 2026-10-23 (docs/storage.md): Datahike's default of 1,000 nodes
 ;; thrashed on a scan of one 115k-datom index (3,704 nodes: a second scan read all again, 270
 ;; ms, as cold), and a cache holding them scanned in 26 ms and opened a sibling branch reading 8
 ;; nodes.
 :seon.config.store/node-cache-bytes 134217728
 ;; Boolean: whether this root is its store's only writer process, so it may collect the store
 ;; (seon.db/collect!; docs/storage.md#collection): Datahike's collection sees only its own
 ;; process's in-flight commits (datahike.gc/gc-storage!), so a commit of another process can
 ;; reuse a value the sweep is deleting. False: root never collects, and the store keeps its
 ;; garbage. Provenance, 2026-10-23 (the store-gc finding, 2026-10-23, not kept): the :scripted profile is
 ;; bin/test's and bin/check's, whose roots (a shard's, a step's) share one store; production
 ;; runs one root per store (task seon.db/two-processes-branch-one-store-at-once).
 :seon.config.store/sole-writer? #profile {:default true :scripted false}

 ;; ==== agents =================================================================================

 ;; Unit: MB. The memory Seon itself may use on the machine it targets: a 16 GB MacBook M1
 ;; (owner, 2026-10-22: "run well on a 16GB Macbook M1"), a quarter of it, leaving the rest to
 ;; the OS, the user's work and what agents run (builds, tests); owner, 2026-10-29: root plus ten
 ;; working agents under about 4 GB. At the bound: seon.check/scale fails when root and the fork
 ;; server (their footprints) and :seon.config.agent/max-running agents would exceed it, each agent
 ;; charged the machine's drop in free plus inactive memory (host_statistics64) from before the
 ;; agents started to their peak, per agent, never the sum of their footprints (docs/cost.md#how-we-measure).
 :seon.config.machine/memory-mb 4096
 ;; Unit: MB. The most physical footprint one MCP relay (bin/mcp, one per Claude session) may
 ;; reach: stock perl measured 6.3 MB peak (`/usr/bin/time -l`, 2026-10-22) where the Seon binary
 ;; it replaced was 729 MB. At the bound: seon.check/mcp-proof fails, so a relay that boots Seon
 ;; again is red.
 :seon.config.machine/relay-mb 32

 ;; ==== the check harness (seon.check) =========================================================

 ;; Unit: ms a check's Flow ping (seon.check/ping) waits for a proc to take its control message,
 ;; which a proc takes between two steps. Provenance: task seon.check/every-wait-is-the-event-it-means
 ;; (2026-10-22); the pings it replaced waited 1000 and 2000 ms and read a miss as nil or
 ;; :unknown, and a check beside a full bin/test must not mistake a slow step for a missing
 ;; proc. At the bound: the proc is recorded {:blocked pid :ping-ms ..} in the check's report
 ;; and the check that read it fails, naming it.
 :seon.config.check/ping-ms 10000
 ;; Unit: ms over which a check measures that something does not happen: a paused agent's
 ;; request unanswered (lifecycle), a paused proc's flood not taken and a paused writer's
 ;; commits held (backpressure). Provenance: the windows it replaced were 300, 400 and 500 ms.
 ;; Load only makes what it measures likelier. At the bound: the measurement is read.
 :seon.config.check/hold-ms 500
 ;; Unit: ms after scale's agents are killed over which it measures the memory the kernel
 ;; returns (:system-available-returned-mb-per-agent, reported, not checked). Provenance: the
 ;; window it replaced. At the bound: the available memory is read.
 :seon.config.check/pages-ms 500
 ;; Unit: ms between the readings scale-work takes of the machine's available memory and of each process
 ;; under root's fork server while its agents work (seon.check/scale-work). Provenance: a footprint
 ;; reading of ~25 processes takes ~100-300 ms; the window is what the test children's peaks are caught
 ;; within, a measurement and never a dependency. At the bound: the reading is taken again.
 :seon.config.check/sample-ms 200
 ;; Unit: runs running at once. Provenance: owner, 2026-10-29 ("10-20 should be fine for users
 ;; with small machines"): an agent costs ~20.6 MB of real memory (bin/check scale, 2026-10-29), so
 ;; ten fit an 8 GB machine beside the user's work (was 4, v1's, when an agent was counted by its
 ;; footprint); task seon.config/agents-at-once-follow-the-machine sizes it from the RAM. A ready task beyond it
 ;; waits for an end; 0 starts nothing new while running work finishes. root's start! may
 ;; pass :max-running for one run; the fact on main says what the run used.
 :seon.config.agent/max-running 10
 ;; Unit: turns a run may take when its task sets none (a task's own
 ;; :seon.config.agent/max-turns wins). At the bound the run ends and the done rule judges it.
 ;; Provenance: v1's live runs measured turns-to-green median 14-40, run 11's p90 32
 ;; (2026-10-18); R4's DeepSeek runs p90 6 with good context. 8 cut ordinary runs below
 ;; v1's median (owner, 2026-10-21: "8 turn cap? wtf? make sensible defaults!"), so the bound
 ;; sits above the measured p90. Tokens are the paid resource; task
 ;; seon.model/budgets-are-data-and-spend-is-measured makes them the budget.
 :seon.config.agent/max-turns 40
 ;; Unit: addresses. What every agent reads beside its task's citations: nothing (the owner, 2026-11-05: an agent's context holds what its
 ;; task needs and its plan cites, not a page every agent got: the 26 section parts of six first messages were read or named by no
 ;; later eval, claude_scratch/context-lab/render-chain-design.md section 4). Written as the agent's own cite entities by its first run on its branch
 ;; (seon.task/agent-cites-tx, seon.agent/begin-run!); the agent changes its own with my.agent/cite! and uncite!.
 :seon.config.agent/cites []
 ;; Unit: path, every agent process's working directory, shared, made at the fork server's boot.
 ;; No repository above it: an agent changes code through its REPL, never git (owner, 2026-10-22:
 ;; "no git in agents"); it reads and writes any file by its absolute path. At the bound: n/a.
 :seon.config.agent/cwd "/tmp/seon-agents"
 ;; Unit: messages in each agent channel's buffer (v1's :seon.config.agent/inbox-size 64).
 ;; Small on purpose: a full channel pushes back on its sender (the reader leaves frames in
 ;; the socket).
 :seon.config.agent/inbox-size 64
 ;; Unit: bytes allocated between an agent's collections (Chez's collect-trip-bytes). Chez's
 ;; default scales with the parent heap (E10); a small trip keeps an agent's garbage, and so
 ;; its private pages, small.
 :seon.config.agent/trip-bytes 4194304
 ;; Unit: bytes an agent may inherit outside Chez's static generation. The fork server freezes its
 ;; heap into static at a fork when main's program moved or, after a collection outside static, its
 ;; live non-static bytes pass this; a freeze keeps what is live then for the server's life, so it is not done every fork
 ;; (measured 2026-10-28: 22.6 MB of static over 20 forks when it was). 16 MiB is what
 ;; seon.agent-test/an-agent-inherits-a-static-heap holds an agent to. At the bound: the next fork freezes.
 :seon.config.agent/inherited-bytes 16777216
 ;; Unit: bytes an agent's heap may grow past what it inherited. Past it an allocation throws
 ;; OutOfMemoryError in the form (an :err item), so a runaway agent cannot take the machine.
 :seon.config.agent/heap-extra-bytes 1073741824
 ;; Unit: bytes of each unix socket's send and receive buffer (root, the fork server and every
 ;; agent). Provenance: T1, 1 MB makes a 1 MB reply cost what the ring's did. At the bound a
 ;; writer waits for the reader (frames are never dropped).
 :seon.config.agent/sock-buf-bytes 1048576
 ;; Unit: engine ticks (procedure calls and loop back-edges: work done, never time) one form
 ;; an agent's model or an outside agent sends may run (Jolt's run-interruptible tick budget).
 ;; Provenance: session AGENTS, measured on Jolt 2026-10-21: a tight loop ~4e9 ticks/s, an
 ;; allocating one ~7.5e8, so this stops a runaway form after ~5-25 s of work. At the bound
 ;; the form answers the typed :seon.repl/ticks-spent and the request goes on. 0: none.
 :seon.config.agent/form-ticks 20000000000
 ;; Unit: bytes. What one form the model, an outside agent or the page sends may allocate before it
 ;; stops with :seon.repl/bytes-spent (jolt.host/run-interruptible's byte budget, checked at its tick
 ;; polls, so a stop can land one poll's allocation past it). Provenance: md-scan #9's 17 model evals
 ;; (the optimization session, 2026-10-29): median 1.5 MB, p90 ~66 MB, max 90 MB; 1 GiB is ~11x the
 ;; largest. The agent's own loop (seon.turn/work!) has none. 0: none. Set to none by the owner's
 ;; ruling, 2026-10-30 ("Please remove the 1GB budget. That's way too low"): md-scan #28's median eval
 ;; allocated 47 MB, a register! or edit! 160-230 MB, and the fixed 1 GiB stopped root's whole-program
 ;; loads, a prove and a heap test; an eval's budget is to come from measured evals (task
 ;; seon.agent/an-evals-budget-comes-from-measured-evals).
 :seon.config.agent/form-bytes 0
 ;; Unit: functions. How many of a form's costliest contracted functions (seon.profile/top)
 ;; its reply item and evaluation record keep, each with its exact calls and estimated ms.
 ;; Provenance: worktree session F2's literal 10, made a key (research row top-fns, 2026-10-22). At the
 ;; bound the rest are left out of the item; the record says nothing of them.
 :seon.config.agent/form-top-fns 10
 ;; Unit: lines. How many lines before and after the changed text seon.repl/edit! answers, so the
 ;; agent sees its edit land without reading the definition again. Provenance: the md-scan bench,
 ;; 2026-10-29 (run 5 reread a 250-line scan after each change); two, as a diff's context reads. At
 ;; the bound the rest of the definition is left out of the answer, its (source f) whole.
 :seon.config.agent/edit-context-lines 2
 ;; Unit: engine ticks ONE test (its :each fixtures included) may run, wherever it runs: an
 ;; agent's turn, the judge, bin/test (seon.proofs/run-tests!). One bound, per test, so a test's
 ;; verdict is a function of the test alone and the same in every place, and a green reused by
 ;; its digest means the same thing everywhere (task seon.proofs/one-prove-path; it replaced a
 ;; per-run bound and a turn's own smaller per-run bound, under which
 ;; a proof could be red in its turn and green at the judge). Larger than a form's: cost proofs
 ;; measure large sizes. Provenance: the per-run judge bound it was (no single in-process test
 ;; needed more); each result records its :ticks. At the bound that test is an :error of
 ;; :seon.test/ticks-spent naming it, and the run goes on. 0: none. Lowered 2026-10-29 (owner: "I don't
 ;; want long running tests period and I want to catch the problems sooner"): 200 G let a looping
 ;; test run ~30 min; the costliest green in-process test recorded 8.3 G ticks (p99 1.9 G, median
 ;; 2 M; 1,186 tests' newest verdicts), so 10 G ends a loop in about a minute and fails none today.
 :seon.config.agent/test-ticks 10000000000
 ;; Unit: engine ticks. The least one test may run: its budget is min(test-ticks, max(this,
 ;; test-ticks-factor x the median ticks its green verdicts measured)), so a test with no green history
 ;; gets this. Provenance: the 45,333 verdicts of 1,194 tests in the results cache (2026-10-29; commit
 ;; message): newest-green ticks median 1.7 M, p90 153 M, p99 1.7 G, max 8.3 G; 4 G is about 1-5 s of work
 ;; (a tight loop ~4e9 ticks/s, an allocating one ~7.5e8) and no test but one runaway (seon.db-test/a-create-that-fails-leaves-no-store,
 ;; 3 greens of 81-147 G) spent more than max(4 G, 10 x its median) at any digest. At the bound that test
 ;; is an :error of :seon.test/ticks-spent naming the budget used. The budget is in every test's digest
 ;; through these three keys and never through the history (docs/merging.md#a-tests-tick-budget).
 :seon.config.agent/test-ticks-floor 4000000000
 ;; Unit: a factor (times). How many times its median green ticks a test may run, at any digest
 ;; (see test-ticks-floor): the same test's greens vary 1.0-1.07 x the median at p50-p90 and 3.3 x at p99
 ;; (44,368 verdicts); 10 is past every test but one runaway above the floor.
 :seon.config.agent/test-ticks-factor 10
 ;; The silence, ms, after which an agent with a request in flight is observed stalled, once per
 ;; quiet spell (seon.agent-proc's stall check), and a test bin/test runs is named STALLED: a
 ;; recorded fact (:seon.agent/stall, an open :stalled problem), never an end. Provenance: the
 ;; guessed tolerance the agent proc held as a var (30 s; a model call answers or streams within
 ;; it). Past it nothing is ended. 0: no check.
 :seon.config.agent/stall-ms 30000
 ;; Unit: bytes. How much of a stalled agent's stderr file its stall evidence keeps as the fiber dump
 ;; (seon.fx/stall-evidence; seon.lib/tail-bytes says how many earlier bytes were left out). Provenance:
 ;; the literal 262144 (256 KiB) the evidence script's `tail -c` held; a dump of a few hundred fibers fits.
 :seon.config.agent/stall-fibers-bytes 262144

 ;; ==== merges =================================================================================

 ;; Unit: merge runs at once. They run together only when the definitions they change are
 ;; disjoint (an octopus merge); 1 is git's one-at-a-time merge queue. Provenance: DECISIONS (root's
 ;; calls on R3's questions): octopus merges stay off until one combined test run of all octopus
 ;; members, the par-200 hang and root memory growth with concurrent merges are fixed. The code
 ;; default had become 8 (b7459c3) with nothing writing the fact (GUARDS active bug 1); boot now
 ;; writes this. An octopus merge is turned on by stating more here (or on main's root entity).
 :seon.config.merge/max-running 1
 ;; Unit: conflicts. A merge run whose merge of main has more is refused
 ;; (:too-many-conflicts); a task's own :seon.config.merge/max-conflicts wins.
 :seon.config.merge/max-conflicts 3
 ;; Unit: merge turns without green. Past it the merge run ends (:merge-turns); a task's
 ;; own :seon.config.merge/max-merge-turns wins.
 :seon.config.merge/max-merge-turns 2
;; Unit: behind runs. A merge run the writer refuses as behind main (main moved under it) is green, so
;; max-merge-turns never counts it and its task ran again without end (docs/merging.md
;; gap 4: a namespace main gained made every merge behind). Past this many behind runs of one merge task the next
;; behind answer holds the task for root with the writer's evidence. Provenance: worktree session
;; a-behind-merge-is-bounded-and-merges-main-first; three reruns are three chances for main to hold still.
;; At the bound: the task is held (my.root/run! releases it, which grants one more try: the count is of all its behind runs).
:seon.config.merge/max-behind 3


 ;; ==== the build ==============================================================================
 ;; Unit: an EDN map, as text: Jolt's compile options for everything a Seon process compiles (its
 ;; build, the test runner, root's and agents' evals). bin/env exports it as JOLT_COMPILER_OPTIONS,
 ;; which Jolt reads into *compiler-options*' root at start (Jolt jolt/image-fn-forms-opt-in).
 ;; {:image-fn-forms false}: Seon writes no state image, so its fn literals register no source for
 ;; one, a third less allocation a compile (task jolt.backend/image-registration-is-opt-in;
 ;; measured 2026-10-23). Read by bin/env from this line, so it stays one line of text. At the
 ;; bound: none; a value that is no EDN map stops every Jolt process at start, naming it.
 :seon.config.build/jolt-compiler-options "{:image-fn-forms false}"

 ;; ==== Seon's own source ======================================================================

 ;; Unit: path. The repository whose source is the program (session LOOP, resources/seon/tasks/
 ;; seon.source.edn): main imports its dirs at boot when it holds none of them, and each merge
 ;; that changes them is written to their files and committed on :seon.config.source/branch.
 ;; Default: SEON_SOURCE_ROOT, which bin/root sets to its own checkout (owner, 2026-10-22: no
 ;; store, build it from source; a store, resume it; the command line overrides), so a root started
 ;; with bin/root builds main from its checkout's source. Unset (bin/test, bin/check, a root a test
 ;; boots): absent, no source is imported and nothing is written. config/local.edn or --set names
 ;; another tree: the main tree's names its merges worktree (bin/worktree --path merges), so
 ;; write-back never edits main's files.
 :seon.config.source/root #env SEON_SOURCE_ROOT
 ;; Unit: path. The checkout the merge gate runs a merge's deferred process tests on
 ;; (seon.fx/run-tests: a worktree of it at the merges head, the candidate's files over it,
 ;; prove's entry there). Provenance: root's ruling (a), 2026-10-23, from step E's dry run (a test
 ;; root importing test/ could not host a checkout, and its gated merges were all refused).
 ;; Absent (as here): root's own source root (:seon.config.source/root as it resolves, the
 ;; standing root's merges tree; seon.root/start! writes it with the config in force), so every
 ;; root with a source gates a merge on the process tests its change reaches, nobody asking. With
 ;; neither (a test root given no source), such a merge is held, its reason naming the tests and
 ;; that no checkout runs them: never merged with them silently unrun.
 :seon.config.source/gate-checkout nil
 ;; Unit: paths under the root, read in this order. Their .clj files are namespaces; the .edn
 ;; files under resources/seon/schemas are schema resources (schemas are program: an agent
 ;; changes one with my.schema/register!, and the merge writes it back; owner 2026-10-21).
 ;; test/ is in: a task's proofs and their fixtures live there, and an agent holds main's
 ;; tests only when main imported them (step E run #11, 2026-10-23: a worktree root without
 ;; test/ forked a design whose proof namespace did not exist, 27 turns of no progress). Only
 ;; a root with a source root imports (bin/root's: SEON_SOURCE_ROOT); a test's root states its
 ;; own dirs. It costs each boot the test forms' evaluation (+5 s a restart, measured
 ;; 2026-10-22; task seon.source/test-namespaces-built-into-the-binary).
 :seon.config.source/dirs ["src" "test" "resources/seon/schemas"]
 ;; Unit: paths under the root, of those above. The program proper: a namespace whose file is under
 ;; one is the program's, one under test/ (a test's, a fixture's, a helper's like seon.check or
 ;; seon.fixtures, whose names do not end in -test) is not (seon.program/program-nss). Read where
 ;; the program is told from its tests: the failure-point law's sites (a namespace with no file,
 ;; an agent's REPL app, is no failure point). Provenance: root, 2026-10-25 (the live round
 ;; 5's big-map request, whose nearest functions were test/ helpers). At the bound: an empty
 ;; vector makes nothing the program's (no law site).
 :seon.config.source/program-dirs ["src"]
 ;; Unit: paths. The checkout's top-level paths no file read and no binary build reads: left out of
 ;; its tree id (seon.fx/tree-id, bin/tree, which reads this line of the shipped file), so a
 ;; commit to them moves no digest and rebuilds nothing. claude_scratch/ is working papers (AGENTS.md
 ;; section 7), and every merge appends its merge-log line there. A path not named is in the
 ;; tree: a new directory reruns what reads the tree, never reuses a stale green. Provenance:
 ;; root, 2026-10-27 (286 of 919 tests rerun after one merge-log line). At the bound: an empty
 ;; vector puts every file in the tree id.
 :seon.config.source/tree-excludes ["claude_scratch"]
 ;; Unit: qualified var names. A test whose reach (seon.program/graph-index, the compiler's
 ;; facts) references one of these reaches a process of Seon's own: an agent (in-agent), a fork
 ;; (lib/fork!), the binary or a root booted for it (binary-sh, through which ts/start-root!
 ;; starts every test root). Such tests run when named or in a merge's verification (bin/prove
 ;; --merge), not in a plain bin/prove (owner, 2026-10-23; root's ruling 2026-10-22: by the reach
 ;; walk, never a hand tag). A shell (clojure.java.shell/sh) is no such process: a git, perl or
 ;; mkfifo call costs milliseconds, and naming sh here left main-test's laws out of every plain
 ;; prove (audit 2026-10-26, item 7). Provenance: step E's run 4, where a turn's tests booted
 ;; roots and spun for minutes in an agent's fork; process tests were 782 s of 917 s of recorded
 ;; test time (pure-tests, 2026-10-23). A var missing here makes its tests run automatically
 ;; (slower, never wrong).
 ;; seon.cost-test/measure-unit and seon.print-cost-test/as-defined! start no process, but measure or reload the runtime
 ;; they run on, which is bin/test's and not the built binary an agent's test child forks from: those proofs run at the gate.
 :seon.config.source/process-vars #{seon.agent-test/in-agent seon.cost-test/measure-unit seon.lib/fork! seon.print-cost-test/as-defined! seon.test-store/binary-sh}
 ;; Unit: vars. What the processes a test starts run for it: a form using a process var or a binary
 ;; var reaches them. seon.server/op-answer, the server's answer, a multimethod on a request's :op:
 ;; a form reaches only the methods of the ops it sends (its :form/ops), so a change to the :done
 ;; answer moves the tests that send :done and no other. seon.turn/work! is the agent's turn loop,
 ;; which root sends an agent as a quoted form, which no reference shows. Provenance: ctx-atom's
 ;; evidence (2026-10-27): seon.server-test's in-agent tests kept one reach digest across two fixes
 ;; to code their agent runs. Not seon.main/-main (1,512 vars): a root's boot is every process
 ;; test's, so each change deferred all 116 to the merge (owner, 2026-10-27: "no tests should
 ;; include all of src … the daily full run is the net"); a change to the boot alone is the merge's
 ;; run's and the daily run's to catch.
 :seon.config.source/process-entry #{seon.server/op-answer seon.turn/work!}
 ;; Unit: submodule paths. The runtime every test, and every check step's contract key, runs on:
 ;; Jolt (its host layer, clojure.core and analyzer, which 970 of 977 tests reach) and Chez under it;
 ;; the binary and the test runner are built from them. Their gitlinks (a dirty submodule's files as
 ;; they are, seon.fx/pin) are in :env, so a bump of either re-runs every test once (about 5 minutes
 ;; for the non-process set). Measured 2026-10-29: seon.cost-test/the-unit-table-is-current stayed
 ;; green across a Jolt pin that moved its numbers, its digest unmoved. Replaces root's ruling D
 ;; (2026-10-27), which gave the runtime core no pin to spare reruns (Jolt bumped 15 times in 3 days)
 ;; and left a change there to the forks' suites and the full run, and the Jolt source groups it keyed
 ;; (analyzer, IR, deps, nREPL, core.async), which a pin in every digest subsumes.
 :seon.config.source/runtime-pins #{"reference-code/jolt" "reference-code/chez"}
 ;; Unit: namespace prefix -> submodules. The libraries a test takes the pin of by what its reach
 ;; references (the namespace's own library and the libraries it runs on): a datahike bump reruns the
 ;; tests that reach datahike.*, not every test. Each submodule is a class (seon.config/pin-class),
 ;; taken by reach alone, by a test that reads files or runs a process too; lmdb and zstd by
 ;; konserve.jolt.lmdb and konserve.jolt.zstd, llama.cpp by seon.vector, awesome-jolt by none.
 ;; Provenance: root's task (2026-10-27): pins moved every digest in 3 of the last 12 --merge
 ;; batches (datahike 3, konserve 2, jolt 2).
 :seon.config.source/pin-namespaces
 {datahike #{"reference-code/datahike" "reference-code/konserve" "reference-code/persistent-sorted-set"
             "reference-code/jolt-fressian" "reference-code/jolt-overlay"}
  konserve #{"reference-code/konserve" "reference-code/jolt-fressian"}
  seon.vector #{"reference-code/llama.cpp"}
  awesome-jolt #{"reference-code/awesome-jolt"}
  malli #{"reference-code/malli"}
  konserve.jolt.lmdb #{"reference-code/lmdb"}
  konserve.jolt.lmdb-ffi #{"reference-code/lmdb"}
  konserve.jolt.zstd #{"reference-code/zstd"}
  jolt.crypto #{"reference-code/crypto"}
  org.replikativ.persistent-sorted-set #{"reference-code/persistent-sorted-set"}
  me.tonsky.persistent-sorted-set #{"reference-code/persistent-sorted-set"}
  fress #{"reference-code/jolt-fressian"}
  hasch #{"reference-code/jolt-overlay"}
  clojure.spec #{"reference-code/jolt-overlay"}}
 ;; Unit: vars. The vars through which a test reaches the built binary (target/seon): a test whose
 ;; reach references one runs it, so bin/test builds it first; a process test that only shells
 ;; out (git) does not. Provenance: root's task seon.check/a-run-builds-the-binary-only-when-
 ;; something-reaches-it (2026-10-23): bin/test built it (46 s) whenever anything ran. A var
 ;; missing here is a missed build (a test fails on a stale binary), so it names the one way a
 ;; test starts the binary: seon.test-store/binary-sh, and seon.vector/start!, which execs
 ;; `target/seon embed-worker` (2026-10-29: the helper tests ran a stale binary, red and green at one
 ;; digest, until this named it).
 ;; seon.test-store/process-store (2026-10-29): a test that reads the process store's path needs the store
 ;; bin/test's plan makes, and the program bin/test seeds on it (seon.root/seed-program!), though it starts
 ;; no process itself (test-store-test's a-test-root-is-its-own failed with no-process-store when only it ran).
 :seon.config.source/binary-vars #{seon.test-store/binary-sh seon.test-store/process-store seon.vector/start!}
 ;; Unit: branch. Merges are committed here, never on main and never pushed (owner). Worktrees share
 ;; refs, so a worktree root writing "jolt/seon-merges" would move the main tree's merges branch:
 ;; a worktree's is its own, jolt/seon-merges-<worktree> (#seon/own-branch, the loader's: task
 ;; seon.root/root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl, gap 1; audit cut 11, 2026-10-27: it was
 ;; an env var bin/env set, outside the config).
 :seon.config.source/branch #seon/own-branch "jolt/seon-merges"
 ;; Unit: branch. Git's main, which worktree branches merge into: a branch's health step reads
 ;; what it caused since its merge base with it (seon.fx/base-commit; root's ruling,
 ;; 2026-10-23). At the bound: n/a.
 :seon.config.source/main "main"
 ;; Unit: a git identity. Seon authors and commits its merges (the message names the merged
 ;; tasks and runs). Stated here because a root's HOME is its checkout's target/home, which
 ;; holds no git identity (bin/check loop, 2026-10-21: "Author identity unknown").
 :seon.config.source/committer-name "Seon"
 :seon.config.source/committer-email "seon@localhost"
 ;; Unit: path. Where each generation is built (seon.fx/build, docs/source.md#the-next-generation):
 ;; <dir>/<commit>, a worktree of the merges commit (bin/worktree --at) with its own target/,
 ;; whose target/seon bin/root next starts. Kept with the run (a generation is a record of what
 ;; ran); one worktree and one ~200 MB binary per merges commit built.
 :seon.config.source/generations-dir #join [#ref [:seon.config.root/runs-dir] "/generations"]
 ;; Unit: path. Where a merge's deferred process tests run (seon.fx/run-tests, root's ruling
 ;; 2026-10-22): <dir>/<sha>-<uuid>, a worktree of the merges head with the candidate's files,
 ;; removed after its run; its log stays beside it (<dir>/<sha>-<uuid>.tests.log).
 :seon.config.source/tests-dir #join [#ref [:seon.config.root/runs-dir] "/merge-tests"]
 ;; Unit: path. Where every checkout's test and check verdicts are kept, one small file per (key,
 ;; digest, status) (seon.fx/write-verdicts!, docs/merging.md#verdicts-are-a-cache): a verdict is derived
 ;; data, a function of the code its test reaches, so it is an evictable cache outside the store (owner,
 ;; 2026-10-28). bin/env sets SEON_RESULTS_DIR to the main tree's target/results (git's common dir), so
 ;; every worktree, the merge gate's checkout and every agent read and write one cache. Unset: this
 ;; checkout's own. At the bound: a missing directory is made by the first write.
 :seon.config.proofs/results-dir #or [#env SEON_RESULTS_DIR #seon/checkout "target/results"]
 ;; Unit: bytes. The whole verdict cache's capacity (the files under results-dir). A write that takes it
 ;; past the bound removes the least recently used verdicts (oldest file mtime; a read touches it),
 ;; greens and reds alike, until it is under, never one just written; below the bound nothing is
 ;; removed. Provenance: the owner, 2026-10-29 (docs/decisions.md); the main tree's cache held 121 MB
 ;; in 27,079 files that day. At the bound: an evicted verdict's test runs again once.
 :seon.config.proofs/results-max-bytes 536870912
 ;; Unit: processes. A proof run's selected tests (bin/prove, bin/test) are dealt, a namespace (with
 ;; its fixtures) at a time, into this many shards, each a runner process of its own that bin/test
 ;; starts at once (seon.proofs/shards), its greens stored by digest when it has run. Provenance:
 ;; worktree session PROVE 2026-10-22 on the 16 GB M1 target (AGENTS section 8): 4 keeps the machine
 ;; responsive beside a standing root and other worktree sessions (measured in docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches).
 ;; At the bound: a run never has more shards; fewer when fewer namespaces are selected. bin/check
 ;; runs this many steps at once too, and mg-check in this many shards (pure-tests 2026-10-23).
 :seon.config.proofs/jobs 4
 ;; Unit: ms. The least work a test child of an agent's test run is worth (seon.agent/run-tests-forked): the run forks
 ;; no more children than its tests' typical ms (seon.fx/typical-ms) divided by this, so a handful of quick tests run in
 ;; one. Provenance: measured 2026-10-29 (load ~10): a run of one trivial test in a fresh child took 36-42 ms (fork,
 ;; child start, exit); a child saves its cost only past twice that on each side, so 100 (docs/merging.md#a-merge-runs-what-the-change-reaches).
 ;; At the bound: a run of less work than this runs in one child.
 :seon.config.proofs/child-min-ms 100
 ;; Unit: ms. What a test counts for in that division when none of the run's tests was ever measured (the mean of those
 ;; measured, when some were). Provenance: the median typical ms of the main tests, 2026-10-29 (a verdict cache read).
 :seon.config.proofs/unmeasured-ms 16
 ;; Unit: boolean. Whether each commit that moves main's program starts a run of main's tests
 ;; (seon.prove: a prove agent forked from main, results as facts every agent forked after reuses).
 ;; Provenance: task seon.proofs/main-is-proved-in-a-fork. Off: the run of all of main's
 ;; in-process tests (344 in one child) dies at a varying test with exit 1 and no stderr, losing
 ;; every result, about 200 s of CPU per commit for nothing; named runs (seon_prove, a turn's)
 ;; are unaffected. On again when task seon.agent/a-test-run-streams-its-reports and the child's
 ;; death are fixed. At the bound: a program commit starts no run.
 ;; Unit: hh:mm UTC. The cron proc's daily full run of main (bin/test --all, which runs every test,
 ;; process tests included, green or not): the net under the narrow digests (a test takes only what
 ;; its reach names, owner 2026-10-27), so what a reach cannot show is found within a day. "" is
 ;; never. Off here because every root with this config would run it, a worktree's too, at once and
 ;; on one machine: the standing root of the main tree sets it (config/local.edn or
 ;; --set :seon.config.proofs/full-run-at="03:00"). A malformed time refuses the config. Provenance:
 ;; root's task (2026-10-27): nothing ran the full run, the narrow digests' only net.
 :seon.config.proofs/full-run-at ""
 :seon.config.proofs/prove-main-on-commit false
 ;; How many times its own last recorded run (ms) a test bin/test runs may be quiet before it is named
 ;; STALLED (bin/stalls): a test with no record, or a bound under :seon.config.agent/stall-ms, takes
 ;; that key. Provenance: at a flat 30 s, 21 healthy process tests were named STALLED in one run (each
 ;; quiet 31-32 s while a root booted or an agent ran, then green); 3x its own time names a hang and
 ;; leaves a slow test. Past it the test is named, never ended.
 :seon.config.proofs/stall-factor 3
 ;; Unit: path. The plan's store of kept program dbs (test-runner/program-db): one LMDB store
 ;; per checkout, a branch per git tree id holding that tree's program as facts. A tree never
 ;; seen is branched from the kept tree with the fewest changed files and brought current by
 ;; seon.source/refresh-tx, so a plan imports the whole program only into an empty store.
 ;; Provenance: owner via root, 2026-10-23 (option B of docs/source.md).
 ;; Every text is read from the tree's git objects, never the working tree. One writer at a time
 ;; (two runs never share a checkout, AGENTS section 3). Derived data: deleting it costs one
 ;; import. At the bound: see program-store-max-bytes.
 :seon.config.proofs/program-store #seon/checkout "target/program-store"
 ;; Unit: bytes. The kept program dbs' store on disk (its data.mdb). Measured 2026-10-23: 12 MB
 ;; for the whole program (118 files), about 2 MB more a kept tree that changed a file. At the
 ;; bound: the store is removed before it is opened and starts again from the run's tree (one
 ;; import); an LMDB file never shrinks, so collecting would not bring it under.
 :seon.config.proofs/program-store-max-bytes 268435456
 ;; Unit: a salt (integer), 0 normally. Salt of every generative check's seed (seon.proofs/seed): a
 ;; seed is derived from what its check reaches (a test's reach digest, a contract's var and the
 ;; binary's tree), so the same digest draws the same cases and a result is a function of its
 ;; digest, while a code change draws new ones. Change it to re-roll every seed once. Provenance:
 ;; worktree session pure-tests 2026-10-23 (mg/check drew from the wall clock; owner via root:
 ;; derive each seed from its digest, not one constant). At the bound: none.
 :seon.config.proofs/seed-salt 0
 ;; Unit: the size n of a cost proof's input (seon.cost/sizes). A proof measures its sizes up to
 ;; this, and at least its two smallest (a growth fit needs two). Provenance: root, 2026-10-23
 ;; (root's test-time measurement, 2026-10-23, not kept: building inputs at 10k took 10-19 s per proof in every run).
 ;; bin/test --all sets SEON_COST_MAX_N=100000, so the full run measures each proof as written.
 ;; At the bound: the proof measures no size past this but its two smallest.
 :seon.config.proofs/cost-max-n #long #or [#env SEON_COST_MAX_N 1000]
 ;; Unit: calls in a row. A cost proof's measure calls the function until this many in a row allocate
 ;; no fewer bytes than the least so far (its steady state: the first calls allocate more as Jolt
 ;; compiles what is hot and caches fill; measured 2026-10-27, seon.merge/delta-three-way at n=300:
 ;; 3,776 2,752 1,536 3,584, 2,752 x8, 1,536, then 320 B over the base, call after call: a run of 8
 ;; equal calls is not the end, so the count is twice that). Provenance: worktree session
 ;; a-call-allocates-the-same-bytes-each-time. At the bound: n/a.
 :seon.config.proofs/cost-settled 16
 ;; Unit: calls. The most calls one measure makes before it takes the last cost-settled as they are
 ;; (a function that never settles: another thread's collection falling in most calls). Provenance:
 ;; worktree session a-call-allocates-the-same-bytes-each-time; the warm-up above settled by call 14, so 30 calls settle it and this is twice that.
 ;; At the bound: the measure answers the least of the calls, marked :unsettled in its meta.
 :seon.config.proofs/cost-max-samples 60
 ;; Unit: test.check sizes. A generated cost proof draws case i at size (mod i this) (seon.cost/cases),
 ;; so a proof of this many cases or more reaches every size from 0 up. Provenance: worktree session
 ;; a-contract-carries-its-cost-bound 2026-10-26 (test.check's sample sizes, 0 to 100). At the bound:
 ;; no case is drawn larger; a bound must hold past it on its own terms, which its :gen can draw.
 :seon.config.proofs/case-sizes 101
 ;; Unit: engine ticks. What one measure of a call's ticks may be off by, whatever the load: Chez
 ;; feeds the timer in chunks of 1,000 ticks, and some calls are charged one whole chunk more
 ;; than others on the same input, plus up to ~50 ticks. Provenance: worktree session store,
 ;; 2026-10-23, task seon.cost/ticks-are-exact: 40 calls of d/db-with on one pinned input read
 ;; 407,097 + 0..46 on 17 and + 1,041..1,048 on 23, with no collection in any of them; the
 ;; chunk's cause in Chez is open on that task. At the bound: seon.cost/judge takes this off a
 ;; size's ticks before judging it over its estimate, and against the growth before fitting it
 ;; (the smallest size's up, the others' down), so one stray chunk is never a red.
 :seon.config.proofs/tick-quantum #long 1050
 ;; Unit: seeds (a count). How many seeds a randomized property test draws in this run, each
 ;; from its own test-seed (seon.proofs/test-seed) and the next ones after it. Provenance: root,
 ;; 2026-10-23 (root's test-time measurement, 2026-10-23, not kept: r3.inc-test's 5 seeds x 150 steps took 47 s, the
 ;; automatic run's critical path). bin/test --all sets SEON_SEEDS=5, so the full run draws as
 ;; many as before. At the bound: the test draws this many and no more.
 :seon.config.proofs/seeds #long #or [#env SEON_SEEDS 1]

 ;; ==== effects and the model ==================================================================

 ;; Unit: requests one effect owner holds queued (its in-port and its priority queue). Each
 ;; agent has at most one synchronous effect in flight, so beyond the live agents only async
 ;; sends queue. At the bound agent procs park (back-pressure). Session FX1.
 :seon.config.fx/buf 1024

 ;; Who answers agents' turns. With no profile, none: the first turn answers the typed
 ;; :seon.model/no-provider and the run ends on it (root's ruling 2026-10-22: production
 ;; ran the scripted test fixture as if it were a model). --profile deepseek (or
 ;; --set :seon.config.ai/provider=:openai-compat) uses the endpoint below; --profile scripted, the
 ;; scripted provider (a test fixture) bin/check and bin/test run under, no paid call: it needs
 ;; that profile, which states its script (--set :seon.config.ai/provider=:scripted alone leaves none, and boot
 ;; refuses the provider's contract). There is no default model (docs/model.md#no-default-model);
 ;; check the context before any paid call.
 :seon.config.ai/provider #keyword #profile {:default "none" :scripted "scripted" :deepseek "openai-compat"}
 ;; The scripted provider's reply function (a test fixture): replies by task.
 :seon.config.ai/script #profile {:scripted seon.fixtures/reply}
 ;; Unit: the provider's own model name. Provenance: v1 owner ruling 2026-09-10, deepseek-flash
 ;; is the default for every agent.
 :seon.config.ai/model #or [#env SEON_AI_MODEL "deepseek-flash"]
 ;; Unit: completion tokens, reasoning included. Provenance: seon.model's default-max-tokens
 ;; (2,048) before this file. At the bound the reply is cut (:truncated?), or
 ;; the reasoning ate the reply (:seon.model/reasoning-ate-reply). v1 ships 65,536 for Flash
 ;; with thinking on (its 4,096 and 8,192 starved reasoning). Ported 2026-10-21 after the first
 ;; DeepSeek agent on Seon's own code: two refinement runs answered empty, reasoning took all
 ;; 2,048. 2026-10-22, step E (root's research, the archived paper agent-context-research-2026-10-22.md (git history) §3.3):
 ;; 8,192 while thinking is off (the default below). The largest ordinary reply measured ~5k;
 ;; loop replies ran for minutes (168 of 181 model-minutes, 12.4M tokens, AUDIT-agents §5.2),
 ;; and a task whose every reply runs to 65,536 costs ~$5.8 (one such reply ~$0.04). A task or
 ;; model that turns thinking on raises it through the resolver (v1's 65,536 for that case).
 :seon.config.ai/max-tokens 8192
 ;; DeepSeek's thinking mode (:enabled | :disabled). Off (owner, 2026-10-21: "if we can't have
 ;; intuitive function calling based on the context our context is bad and instructions need
 ;; help"): a task whose next step needs long reasoning is a context or instruction defect to
 ;; fix, not a cost to pay every turn. A model or task can turn it on through the resolver
 ;; (task -> model -> provider -> config); effort is :seon.config.ai/reasoning-effort.
 :seon.config.ai/thinking :disabled
 ;; Whether a request sends earlier turns' stored reasoning back. Unit: flag. Provenance: owner
 ;; ruling 2026-10-24 (docs/decisions.md, "A config key does what it says"). true: every earlier
 ;; reply's reasoning goes back exactly as it was returned (seon.model/request-body); false: it
 ;; is left out of the request, and still stored on the turn and shown. With thinking :enabled
 ;; and tools (every request) false is refused at load (:seon.config.ai/reasoning-pair):
 ;; DeepSeek answers 400 when it is left out (measured 2026-10-24, docs/model.md).
 ;; With thinking off there is no reasoning to send.
 :seon.config.ai/replay-reasoning true
 ;; Unit: USD. One first-move pass's spend (my.root/first-moves!: one call per task, ~6k
 ;; prompt tokens at turn 0, ~$0.001 a task off-peak, twice at DeepSeek's peak); at the bound
 ;; the pass stops and writes :seon.judge/stopped. 0.10 is about a hundred tasks' first moves,
 ;; out of the owner's standing $50 (docs/model.md#paid-runs).
 :seon.config.ai/judge-pass-usd 0.10
 ;; Unit: model calls in flight at once, cluster-wide: our admission cap, the :model owner's
 ;; workers (session FX1). At the bound turns queue in the owner, by priority. Our choice, small:
 ;; eight is twice the runs running at once (:seon.config.agent/max-running 4), so a
 ;; merge's or a judge's call never waits behind the work, while a runaway loop spends at most
 ;; eight calls' tokens at a time. The provider's own ceiling, per account across all keys
 ;; (api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/rate_limit, read 2026-10-21): 2,500 concurrent for
 ;; deepseek-flash, 500 for deepseek-v4-pro; far above this, so the cap is ours, not theirs.
 :seon.config.ai/max-in-flight 8

 ;; ---- the model manifest (v1's config/default.edn initialization rows, ported for the models
 ;; we use). A setting resolves task -> model -> its provider -> this file's top-level key
 ;; (seon.config/resolve, the one way code reads a setting); root writes each provider and model
 ;; as an entity on main (platform records). A model or provider states a setting by its
 ;; :seon.config.* key to override the top-level one for itself.
 :seon.config.ai/providers
 {"deepseek"
  {;; Unit: URL; /chat/completions is appended (v1's :seon.config.ai/endpoint named the whole URL).
   :seon.config.ai/base-url "https://api.deepseek.com"
   ;; Unit: the NAME of an environment variable. Never the credential: root's :model owner reads
   ;; the key at the call, it never becomes a datom, and every agent unsets this variable at its
   ;; birth (the key is root's alone).
   :seon.config.ai/api-key-variable "DEEPSEEK_API_KEY"
   ;; the request field the completion budget is sent in (v1)
   :seon.ai.model/output-token-wire-key "max_tokens"
   ;; Unit: tokens. The block DeepSeek's prompt cache counts a hit in. Provenance: measured
   ;; 2026-10-25 on 100 recorded turns of 6 runs (.worktrees/live-agents): every
   ;; prompt_cache_hit_tokens a multiple of 128. At the bound: a turn's hit more than one block
   ;; short of the prefix it shares with the turn before is a :seon.turn/cache-miss.
   :seon.config.ai/cache-block-tokens 128}}
 ;; Prices: USD per million tokens, off-peak; peak (01:00-04:00 and 06:00-10:00 UTC, weekdays,
 ;; not Chinese public holidays) is twice. Read 2026-10-21 from
 ;; api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/pricing. Concurrency ceilings: the provider's, per account
 ;; across all keys, read 2026-10-21 from api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/rate_limit; our own
 ;; cap is :seon.config.ai/max-in-flight.
 :seon.config.ai/models
 {"deepseek-flash"
  {:seon.ai.model/provider "deepseek"
   ;; DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017; the legacy names deepseek-v4-flash(-vision-exp) route here
   :seon.ai.model/version "DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017"
   :seon.ai.model/input-usd-per-mtok 0.15
   :seon.ai.model/cached-input-usd-per-mtok 0.003
   :seon.ai.model/output-usd-per-mtok 0.6
   :seon.ai.model/source "api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/pricing and /rate_limit, read 2026-10-21"}
  "deepseek-v4-pro"
  {:seon.ai.model/provider "deepseek"
   :seon.ai.model/version "DeepSeek-V4-Pro-0813"
   :seon.ai.model/input-usd-per-mtok 0.66
   :seon.ai.model/cached-input-usd-per-mtok 0.022
   :seon.ai.model/output-usd-per-mtok 1.98
   :seon.ai.model/source "api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/pricing and /rate_limit, read 2026-10-21"}}
 ;; Unit: tokens per second the provider admits, cluster-wide: the :model owner's token bucket
 ;; (session R3; AIMD on a 429). 0: no bucket.
 :seon.config.ai/rate 0
 ;; Unit: tokens. The block a provider's prompt cache stores a prefix in, stated by each provider
 ;; that caches (DeepSeek's: 128). 0 here: a provider that states none has no prompt cache known,
 ;; and no turn is checked against one.
 :seon.config.ai/cache-block-tokens 0
 ;; Unit: characters per token, the one estimate the printer and the context size with.
 ;; Provenance: v1's :seon.config.ai/chars-per-token-prior, 17 DeepSeek prompt samples
 ;; (2026-08-08/10) fit 3.21-3.23.
 :seon.config.ai/chars-per-token-prior 3.2

 ;; ==== the printer (seon.print: every value a person or a model reads) ========================

 ;; Unit: tokens a reply's value may spend, wherever an eval's value is shown (the model's
 ;; tool message, the agent's page). Provenance: v1's 6144 (owner 2026-10-12: print more, so
 ;; help and the current-state block show whole), raised to 16000 (owner 2026-10-25: "a
 ;; generous context budget"; main's problems through their render measured 14,218 tokens
 ;; whole, cut at 6144; docs/render.md). Past it the value is cut, every
 ;; cut carrying the form that reads its rest.
 :seon.config.render.agent/token-budget 16000
 ;; Unit: nested levels shown (v1's :seon.config.render.agent/max-depth).
 :seon.config.render.agent/max-depth 8
 ;; Unit: characters. A value whose laid-out text is at most this wide is never cut: its cut
 ;; would say less than the value (v1).
 :seon.config.render.agent/whole-width 24
 ;; Unit: characters. A keyword or symbol is one name, whole up to this length (v1).
 :seon.config.render.agent/max-name 256
 ;; Unit: decimal digits. A BigInt, Ratio or BigDecimal past this prints as its digit count (v1).
 :seon.config.render.agent/max-number-digits 32
 ;; Unit: keys. A keyword-keyed map or set of at most this many shows identity attributes and
 ;; short members first, then alphabetically; a larger one its own seq order (v1).
 :seon.config.render.agent/sort-bound 64
 ;; Unit: characters per printed line (session P1's printer, matching v1's corpus output).
 :seon.config.render/width 72
 ;; Unit: tokens for a value shown inside another: a refused argument, a test's expected and
 ;; actual, ex-data (session P1).
 :seon.config.render/part-budget 256
 ;; Unit: tokens for a form echoed back (session P1).
 :seon.config.render/form-budget 600
 ;; Unit: tokens for the context block the model reads (the task, its proofs, what it cites),
 ;; breadth first so every section shows before any one deepens (session P1).
 :seon.config.render/context-budget 16000
 ;; Unit: members. The newest members of each collection root's own record holds (its agents, and
 ;; their runs and evals), read newest first and stopping there, so root's long-lived copy of other
 ;; processes' data costs its window, not their history (and the run records, which go: task
 ;; seon.agent/the-agent-is-the-unit-not-the-run). An agent's own record holds every eval, turn and
 ;; message it has, never windowed (the owner, 2026-10-30: "deliver all messages and
 ;; errors"; 2026-10-29: no cuts on an agent's context). Older members stay on main, read by a query.
 :seon.config.ctx/window 100
 ;; Unit: forms. The newest forms naming a cited var (its callers) a cite's record holds, each with the
 ;; vars it defines: the cite's part shows its tests and other callers among them (seon.render.ui/cite-model;
 ;; newer callers of other kinds can push a test out of the window they share). At the bound the older ones
 ;; are left out, the part names (my.agent/tests-reaching 'x), and
 ;; (my.agent/callers 'x) reads them all. 50: the six capped design-build runs of
 ;; 2026-10-26 lacked the cited vars' existing tests (short-history spent 25 turns before finding
 ;; kept's tests, mixed 9 guessing inputs that problem-rows in print_test builds); a cited var is
 ;; named by few forms (a task's own functions), and a hot one (clojure.core/let, 1,600 forms) is cut
 ;; here, its forms read and ordered by when each was written. Measured over this checkout's 830
 ;; tasks citing vars: 97 of the cited vars' 1,430 tests fall past 50 (12 hot helpers, seon.test-store/test-db
 ;; the most), 801 past 5.
 :seon.config.ctx/callers 50
 ;; Unit: sections. The wiki sections that cite a var a task cites, shown with it (its :sections, seon.doc/
 ;; citing-sections: the addresses, to read with (my.agent/help address)): the sections it owes an edit to
 ;; and the ones that explain it. Provenance: root's coverage gap 7 (2026-10-27): wiki sections reached the
 ;; agent only through the task's own citations. At the bound: 0 shows none.
 :seon.config.ctx/cited-by 3
 ;; Unit: tokens of a form's :form/src as the model reads it (seon.print.emit/chars-tokens, the printer's
 ;; one estimate), src and test alike: about a printed page. A live form longer than this is an open
 ;; :long-form problem row (seon.problems/long-forms), shown to a branch that adds one (attention, never
 ;; refused) and closed by splitting it; its task is seon.template/a-finding-is-a-task. Provenance:
 ;; measured over this program (commit 3fac2a4f04): 2,082 src forms, 83 over 1,000 tokens and 20 over
 ;; 2,000 (seon.ref/scan 6,005); 2,052 test forms, 76 over 1,000.
 :seon.config.shrink/form-tokens 1000
 ;; Relevance (seon.context/relevance, docs/vector.md#relevance): a logistic over five features of
 ;; an item near a context, one weight set a kind: zc its cosine with the query's vector, zb its BM25
 ;; for the query's words, over the pool (seon.doc/bm25), zt its cosine with the touched items' mean vector (each a z-score over
 ;; the pool), g1 log(1 + the seeds it neighbours x its rarity), ppr log(1 + scale x its
 ;; personalized PageRank from the seeds), the constants :seon.config.render/relevance-features. Weighing a link by its rarity took plumbing
 ;; (seon.contract/checked, seon.db/eid ...) from 20 to 1 of 570 function lines at the same recall. Provenance: fitted (scikit-learn, L2 C=1) on 220 merges' commit subjects as queries
 ;; and what each merge changed as the relevant items, cold and with three of them touched;
 ;; judged on 57 merged tasks' plans held out (the relevance-lab worktree, 2026-10-27). They hold
 ;; only with :seon.config.render/relevance-features as fitted: change one, fit them again
 ;; (docs/vector.md/). At the bound: none; each kind's set is required.
 :seon.config.render/relevance-weights {:var {:zc 0.382 :zb 0.568 :zt 0.267 :g1 1.527 :ppr 0.112 :intercept -3.881}
                                        :section {:zc 0.162 :zb 0.816 :zt 0.101 :g1 0.690 :ppr 0.255 :intercept -4.100}
                                        :schema {:zc 0.517 :zb 0.318 :zt 0.332 :g1 0.382 :ppr 0.435 :intercept -4.991}}
 ;; The constants relevance's features were fitted with (seon.context/relevance): :alpha the share a
 ;; PageRank push keeps (the field's 0.15, Andersen, Chung and Lang 2006); :bm25-k1 and :bm25-b
 ;; BM25's saturation and length normalization (the field's 1.2 and 0.75, Robertson and Zaragoza
 ;; 2009); :rarity-cap the degree a link's rarity, (1 + cap)/(1 + degree), counts to (16, as the
 ;; rank fusion's measured cap was: no plumbing lines at 16, 64 and 256 alike), which is also the most
 ;; datoms that count reads; :ppr-scale the rank's scale inside its log (10^4: a rank of 1e-4, a
 ;; seed's typical neighbour, reads log 2); :hub the degree from which a node never pushes and is
 ;; never read whole (64 ranked the 57 held-out plans exactly as no bound, while the bound keeps a
 ;; push from counting seon.contract/checked's thousands of callers: each datom read costs ~1.4 KB
 ;; here). Provenance: the weights were fitted with these; no others were measured but :hub's.
 ;; At the bound: none; each is required.
 :seon.config.render/relevance-features {:alpha 0.15 :bm25-k1 1.2 :bm25-b 0.75 :rarity-cap 16 :ppr-scale 10000.0 :hub 64}
 ;; Unit: [logit chance] knots a kind, read piecewise linearly. What a relevance logit means: the
 ;; chance an item is worth reading for the work now, as an independent judge (Sonnet) labelled 1,905
 ;; lines useful or noise: the first five of each kind on 59 merged tasks' pages and 20 search
 ;; queries (the plan's focus, 1,185 lines), and at 48 moments of 10 recorded runs under the work's
 ;; focus (the plan decaying into the agent's evals, 720 lines; docs/vector.md/). Fitted per kind
 ;; as sigmoid(a log c + b) over both, c the item's chance that its merge changes it, and cross-fitted
 ;; by halves of the moments: at 60% or more the plan's pages predicted 0.78 / 0.82 / 0.73 and found
 ;; 0.76 / 0.86 / 0.70 (functions, sections, schema keys), the work's 0.64 / 0.85 / 0.71 and found
 ;; 0.76 / 0.81 / 0.67; a term for the state moved nothing at the cut. Below each kind's fifth item it
 ;; is extrapolated: no label is there. At the bound: a logit past either end reads its end's chance.
 :seon.config.render/relevance-calibration {:var [[-6.51 0.16] [-5.17 0.169] [-3.83 0.243] [-3.3 0.312] [-2.78 0.362] [-1.95 0.433] [-1.12 0.485] [-0.6 0.554] [-0.07 0.602] [0.62 0.701] [1.32 0.753] [2.56 0.77] [3.81 0.784]]
                                            :section [[-6.16 0.0] [-4.98 0.0] [-3.8 0.002] [-3.3 0.004] [-2.81 0.007] [-2.63 0.022] [-2.45 0.045] [-2.19 0.127] [-1.92 0.232] [-1.55 0.373] [-1.19 0.497] [-0.94 0.582] [-0.69 0.651] [-0.54 0.765] [-0.4 0.834] [2.12 0.913] [4.65 0.948]]
                                            :schema [[-8.67 0.014] [-6.58 0.02] [-4.49 0.043] [-4.14 0.07] [-3.79 0.096] [-3.36 0.17] [-2.93 0.237] [-2.39 0.401] [-1.84 0.515] [-1.68 0.591] [-1.52 0.648] [-0.61 0.72] [0.29 0.769] [0.65 0.769] [1.0 0.769]]}
 ;; Unit: chance (0-1). A hit my.agent/search ranks at this relevance or more is shown, a line with its
 ;; chance; below it, counted (a last :more entry) with the call that lists it. A task may set its own (its
 ;; :seon.task/config). Provenance: the judge's labels, cross-fitted: at 0.4 the lines shown were
 ;; worth reading 0.57 (functions), 0.66 (sections) and 0.68 (schema keys) of the time, about 3.6,
 ;; 2.3 and 1.6 a page of the first five; at 0.05 (the merge-change scale's cut before) functions
 ;; were starved and sections and keys half noise (docs/vector.md#relevance). At the bound: an item at
 ;; exactly this chance is shown.
 :seon.config.render/relevance-summary 0.4
 ;; Unit: items of each kind. How many of the nearest by meaning relevance's pool takes
 ;; (seon.vector/near), beside the seeds and their neighbourhood, and how many of a seed's
 ;; neighbours each read of who names it takes (seon.context/neighbours: a plan citing
 ;; seon.config/value would pool its hundreds of callers). Provenance: 100, 200 and 400 ranked
 ;; alike on the held-out plans (functions R@5 0.433 / 0.436 / 0.451, sections 0.764 / 0.766 /
 ;; 0.766); the pool's texts are tokenized each call, so the smallest. At the bound: an item past
 ;; it and outside the seeds' first neighbours is never scored.
 :seon.config.render/relevance-pool 100
 ;; Unit: residual mass per neighbour. The local push's threshold for relevance's personalized
 ;; PageRank (seon.context/pushed): at most 1/(alpha x this) pushes, whatever the store's size.
 ;; Provenance: 1e-3 pushed 37 nodes a plan and ranked as well as 1e-4 (376 nodes; functions R@5 0.434
 ;; and 0.430 on the held-out plans). At the bound: a node holding less than this per neighbour is not pushed.
 :seon.config.render/relevance-eps 0.001
 ;; Unit: hits. How many hits (my.agent/search) answers when its options give no :k: the best of the
 ;; exact text, meaning and references fused by seon.vector/rrf. Provenance: the literal 10 that
 ;; my.agent/similar took (owner, 2026-10-27: "fix the search so it includes docs ... params to
 ;; specify what to focus on"). At the bound: the hits past it are not answered; ask a larger :k.
 :seon.config.render/search 10
 ;; Unit: standard deviations. my.agent/search keeps a text hit (BM25 over the query's identifier-split
 ;; words) only when its score is this many standard deviations above the mean of every text's score for
 ;; that query. Provenance: embeddings lab, 2026-10-27 (20 hand-checked queries; scratchpad
 ;; embeddings-design.md): tokens kept at z >= 2 lifted R@10 from 0.68 to 0.71 without hurting the
 ;; paraphrase queries; unfiltered they are noise on prose. At the bound: a text scoring below it is not a hit.
 :seon.config.render/search-token-z 2.0
 ;; Unit: candidates per hit shown. How many of the nearest by meaning my.agent/search reads for each hit
 ;; it may show (seon.vector/near takes this times :seon.config.render/search), before the
 ;; fusion and the filters cut them to the count. Provenance: the literal the
 ;; fused search was measured with (b9920ecf5b, docs/vector.md#what-was-compared-and-what-it-left-out); no other value
 ;; was measured. At the bound: a candidate past it is never ranked by meaning.
 :seon.config.render/related-near 4
 ;; Semantic search's pyramid (seon.vector/near, docs/vector.md#the-pyramid), measured against an
 ;; exact 768-dim search, recall@10 (EmbeddingGemma-2):
 ;; - bin/check vectors, Seon's whole text (2,681: 1,783 forms with the schemas, 321 sections, the
 ;;   task plans), 207 queries, coarse 128: shortlist 50 0.946, 100 0.979, 200 0.990, 400 0.998;
 ;; - the 1,393 src forms alone: coarse 64 + 100 0.945, coarse 128 + 100 0.9975, fine 256 at most 0.83.
 ;; near in Jolt on 1,737 vectors: shortlist 100 1.9 ms, 200 3.0 ms, 400 4.6 ms (the datom reads);
 ;; the C levels alone 0.08 ms at 1.4k, 0.31 ms at 100k.
 ;; Unit: dims. Stored per vector (seon.vector/pack): a change re-packs every vector.
 :seon.config.vector/coarse-dims 128
 ;; Unit: vectors. At the bound the rest of the coarse ranking is never reranked.
 :seon.config.vector/shortlist 400
 ;; Unit: dims. 768 is the whole vector; fewer reads less and ranks a prefix.
 :seon.config.vector/fine-dims 768
 ;; The embedding model (docs/vector.md#the-model): EmbeddingGemma-2, ggml-org's Q8_0 GGUF, in
 ;; process through llama.cpp v0.7.0 (bin/build embed fetches it once by its sha256 into the main
 ;; tree's target/cache; bin/env alone names it, so its pin is written once). Measured on Seon's 1,393 forms (M5 Max, Metal, load ~10):
 ;; 12 ms one text, 37k tokens/s batched (175 texts/s), 0.86 GB RSS; the BF16 file no faster (34k).
 :seon.config.vector/model #env SEON_EMBED_MODEL
 :seon.config.vector/lib #seon/checkout "target/lib/libseon-embed.dylib"
 ;; Unit: tokens. One encode's context and micro-batch: 4096 measured best of 512-4096 (512 texts/s
 ;; 122, 1024 167, 2048 173, 4096 176), as v1 found (cap tokens, not texts).
 :seon.config.vector/batch-tokens 4096
 ;; Unit: texts. One encode's sequences; at the bound the next text starts the next encode.
 :seon.config.vector/batch-texts 64
 ;; Unit: threads. ggml's CPU pool in the :embed helper (seon.vector/serve!): Metal does the encode, so 1
 ;; measured as fast as 18 (139 vs 140 texts/s, 2026-10-23). Whatever it is, the open model keeps 4
 ;; threads of its own (Metal's), and loading it runs ~32 that end when it has loaded; all in the
 ;; helper's process, none in root's.
 :seon.config.vector/threads 1
 ;; Unit: bytes. How much of the embedding helper's stderr a :seon.vector/helper-died error keeps (its last
 ;; bytes; seon.lib/tail-bytes says how many earlier ones were left out). Provenance: a screenful of llama.cpp's log.
 :seon.config.vector/stderr-bytes 4096
 ;; Unit: tokens. A text past it keeps its first max-tokens (the count cut is recorded). Provenance:
 ;; v1's 2,048 (forms p99 1,766 tokens); at 512, 148 of Seon's 1,393 forms were cut.
 :seon.config.vector/max-tokens 2048
 ;; Unit: texts. One request of main's backlog (seon.vector/vectors-proc): at 175 texts/s a round
 ;; holds the worker ~0.4 s, so a query waits behind at most one round. 0 embeds nothing and the
 ;; model never loads: bin/test's and bin/check's roots (SEON_EMBED_TEXTS), which never search.
 :seon.config.vector/texts #long #or [#env SEON_EMBED_TEXTS 64]
 ;; Unit: requests. What one :embed step takes from the queue (seon.fx :batch): queries queued
 ;; behind a round are embedded together.
 :seon.config.vector/batch 16
 ;; Unit: ranks. Reciprocal rank fusion's k (Cormack et al. 2009 use 60; v1 used 10): measured on
 ;; the 283 pairs, 10 gave recall@10 0.451 and 60 0.448. Read by seon.vector/rrf.
 :seon.config.vector/rrf-k 10
 ;; Unit: bytes. An evaluation that allocated more is flagged on its card and row
 ;; (seon.render.ui/alloc-span). Provenance: render.ui's BIG-ALLOC literal (10 MiB).
 :seon.config.render/big-alloc 10485760
 ;; Unit: tokens a rendered value spends when its env names no budget (seon.render/print-html,
 ;; an agent's history row). Provenance: the literal 600 render.clj and
 ;; render.ui carried (2026-10-22), kept as it was.
 :seon.config.render/value-budget 600
 ;; Unit: tokens of root's context, its page's hiccup printed (seon.render.proc/pass: the render
 ;; of root's ctx, /root/debug and /context.txt); past it the printer names what it left. Provenance: render.ai's
 ;; and the render proc's 700.
 :seon.config.render/root-context-budget 700
 ;; Unit: tokens of a message or reason shown in one line (root's context, a page's tile or row,
 ;; a nested value's line); past it the line's cut names the form that reads the
 ;; rest. Provenance: the literals 40-60 render.ui and render.ai carried (2026-10-22), as one.
 :seon.config.render/line-budget 50
 ;; Unit: tokens of a table cell or a stream row's field (a form, a value, a reason); past it the
 ;; cut says what it omitted and links the page that shows it whole. Provenance: the literals
 ;; 12-30 render.ui carried (2026-10-22), as one.
 :seon.config.render/cell-budget 25
 ;; Unit: events root's context keeps (the render proc), newest first; its events section says
 ;; how many older ones it did not keep. Provenance: the render proc's EVENT-CAP 60.
 :seon.config.render.list/stream 60
 ;; Unit: rows (an evaluation, or a turn with its evaluations). A page renders this many of its
 ;; list at first, newest first, and this many more each time its last row scrolls into view
 ;; (seon.render.proc/debug-value); the rest are counted, never rendered until looked at. Provenance: the
 ;; render proc's HISTORY-CAP 100, then 20 when only what is on screen is rendered (a screen
 ;; holds about 10 rows).
 :seon.config.render.list/history 20

 ;; ==== the web, instrumentation, the native probe ==============================================

 ;; Unit: TCP port on 127.0.0.1 (session W2). 0 (the shipped value): no dashboard. A checkout's
 ;; own config/local.edn (the standing root's --config layer) names its port (bin/ports writes it: the main tree 7777, worktree k
 ;; 7901+10k, from one registry), so no two checkouts share one (owner, 2026-10-21). bin/check
 ;; and bin/test set SEON_WEB_PORT=0 and give their roots no local.edn, so check steps listen on nothing.
 ;; A taken port leaves the web proc {:listening false} on the health line and boot goes on.
 :seon.config.web/port #long #or [#env SEON_WEB_PORT 0]
 ;; Unit: TCP port on 127.0.0.1: root's server (seon.server), which bin/mcp and outside
 ;; agents reach. Three values: >0 that port; 0 (the shipped value) none, `seon root` refuses to
 ;; serve; -1 a free port the kernel assigns. The port bound is named on the root's ROOT LISTENING
 ;; line and recorded in runs/root.port (a test's root: seon.test-store/start-root!,
 ;; never a port picked and released beforehand, which another process can take). config/local.edn names
 ;; the checkout's own (main 7888, worktree k 7900+10k).
 ;; bin/root PORT WEB, bin/mcp PORT and the mcp check set SEON_SERVER_PORT, which local.edn's
 ;; #env honours where bin/root names it (until a built binary's top-level code sees its argv,
 ;; so --set can say it).
 :seon.config.root/server-port #long #or [#env SEON_SERVER_PORT 0]
 ;; datastar.js, read once.
 :seon.config.web/public-dir #seon/checkout "resources/public"
 ;; The page's stylesheet, bin/build's css step's output (Tailwind over
 ;; resources/public/css/input.css, docs/render.md#tailwind); read per request, so a build's
 ;; new stylesheet shows on reload. Missing (no build yet): the page is served unstyled.
 :seon.config.web/stylesheet #seon/checkout "target/css/app.css"
 ;; Unit: +hhmm. Jolt has no time zones: clocks on the page add this offset (`date +%z`).
 ;; Unset: UTC, which the page's clocks then show.
 :seon.config.web/utc-offset "+0000"

 ;; Contracts and their recording (session G1): :on, :contracts (wrappers, no recording
 ;; transaction: the two measured apart), :off (no wrapper, no recording: the same binary
 ;; measured with instrumentation off; test/r3's benches).
 :seon.config.contract/instrument #keyword #or [#env SEON_INSTRUMENT "on"]

 ;; native/probe.c's library (bin/build native): Mach memory probes and qfork. T5 points it at
 ;; next's build from a snapshot that has none.
 :seon.config.native/probe-lib #seon/checkout "target/lib/libprobe.dylib"

 ;; The SQLite seon.sqlite binds for the text index's FTS5 table (docs/vector.md#search): macOS's own, which
 ;; the system holds in its shared cache (the path has no file, dlopen finds it) and builds with FTS5 and
 ;; Porter's tokenizer. Unit: path. Missing, or without FTS5: the first text index throws
 ;; :seon.sqlite/unloaded or :seon.sqlite/refused. Provenance: the task seon.doc/the-text-index-is-fts5.
 :seon.config.native/sqlite-lib "/usr/lib/libsqlite3.dylib"}
